    "Byun Sungkyu (pen name Seokjeong, 1890-1962) learned to paint as a hobby while working as a doctor at his Oriental medicinal clinic in Daegu. His paintings encompassed a range of themes, including the four gracious plants, still life with vessels and other objects, landscape with mountains and water, and portraits, as well as birds and flowers. Being a talented calligrapher, Byun also created signboards for many pavilions in the Gyeongsangbuk-do region as seen in the representative example Ayangnu Pavilion. Byun’s works can be found in various places throughout Daegu, and they are also pieces held at the Calligraphy Museum of Korea. Byun is also known for being the father of Byun Chongja (1926-2000), the famed Western-style painter.\n The theme of “still-life with vessels, plants, and fruits” refers to a type of traditional painting made for enjoyment or decoration, featuring a literary figure’s choice of different vessels, flowers, fruits, stones, or writing supplies. For his Still-Life with Vessels, Plants and Fruits, Byun filled eight rectangular panels with diagonal arrangements of objects that include bronze vessels and decorative pots of apricot flowers, lilies, bamboo, and pines. On the right-hand side of each panel are calligraphic writings related to the featured flower or tree.\n": 1,
    "Kwon Jinho (1915-1951) graduated from Daegu Agricultural School, and his work was selected for the 13th Joseon Art Exhibition in 1934. In 1935, he finished the course of education at Daegu Normal School and became an elementary school teacher in Yeongju. Serving as a teacher, he kept producing artworks, and his works are accepted at both the 18th and the 19th Joseon Art Exhibitions.\n  A Girl depicts a young woman sitting in chair against a piano. Wearing in red jeogori, she has a round head and eyes, full lips, and rosy cheeks that show her young age. “Girl in hanbok” was a popular subject in the Korean art field from the 1930s to the 1950s. This results from the pursuit of local colors at that time. Written on the lower part of the screen is “Shōwa 17 (昭和 十七年) Higashi Koichi painted (東浩一 写).” Shōwa refers to the period of Japanese history from 1926 to 1989, and Higashi Koichi is Kwon’s Japanese name. In other words, these words imply that Kwon created this work in 1942 (Shōwa 17). ": 1,
    "Kim, Jongchan(1916-?) graduated from the Soongin Middle School in Pyongyang, and went to the Department of Western-style painting in Imperial Art Institute(帝国美術学校), Tokyo, Japan. From 1939, he studied at Culture Institute(文化学院), and submitted the works to Nikaten(二科展), which was one of the authoritative exhibitions. During the Pacific War, he served as a war artist for Japanese military, and participated in Sacred War Art Exhibition(聖戦美術展).\n\nSince Kim’s works do not remain a lot, it is difficult to know his artistic style. Yet depending on a few surviving works, he seemed to adopt Academism and Impressionism.\n\nThis work was submitted to the 1st exhibition of Joseon New Artist Association(Joseon Sinmisulga Hyeop-hoe) in Tokyo, and the postcard of this work remains as a material. It depicts the scenery of a pond in which the lotus and its leaves cover, and two ducks enjoy swimming. Kim lightly used oil paints to give a plain sense, like ink-and-wash painting. This can be seen as the artist’s try to reveal the Korean aesthetics. Although this painting is small-size work, it reveals Kim’s skill.\n": 1,
    "Kim, Jongchan(1916-?) graduated from Soongin Middle School in Pyongyang, and went to the Department of Western-style painting in Imperial Art Institute(帝国美術学校), Tokyo, Japan. From 1939, he studied at Culture Institute(文化学院), and submitted the works to Nikaten(二科展), which was one of the authoritative exhibitions. During the Pacific War, he served as a war artist for Japanese military, and participated in Sacred War Art Exhibition(聖戦美術展).\n Since Kim’s works do not remain a lot, it is difficult to know his artistic style. Yet depending on a few surviving works, he seemed to adopt Academism and Impressionism.\n Kim painted this work in 1939 when he built a successful career, submitting his works to Nikaten. He expressed the hut as a old and shabby building by the colors and textures which are made by layering the brownish colors several times. The desolate scenery, and a boy crouching down in front of the hut reflect the colonial era of Korea. ": 1,
    "Kim, Chongtai(pen name: Hoesan, 1906-1935) taught himself Western-style painting. He was accepted at the 5th Joseon Art Exhibition, and received the fourth consecutive special prizes in the exhibition. Such achievements make him be selected as the first Korean recommended artist in that exhibition. He went to Japan for study in 1930, and his work was accepted at Nikaten(二科展) in 1934.\n Kim painted objects with color block and one quick stroke of a brush. Criticizing the works of artists studying abroad without Korean local colors, he emphasized that they have Korean aesthetic consciousness.\n This work, Kim’s only landscape painting, depicts Mt. Juam in Pyongyang, North Korea, at nightfall. Since the sun was setting, the clouds and river have the light orange colors. The scenery is overall static and silent, and the sailboat, which is crossing the river, highlights tranquility. This work shows Kim’s characteristic brushwork, which is one quick stroke of a brush.": 1,
    "Kim, Junghyun(pen name: Cheolma, 1901-1953) taught himself painting. Around 1925, he worked at the Department of Land Survey in the Japanese Government-General of Korea, and created paintings in his spare time. He received numerous prizes from Joseon Art Exhibition, steadily submitting his works from the 4th exhibition in 1925 to the 22nd exhibition in 1943. He was awarded two special prizes from the Departments of Eastern-style painting, and Western-style painting at the 15th exhibition in 1936. After Korea’s liberation from Japan in 1945, he served as a president in Joseon Commercial Artist Association(Joseon Sangeop Misulga Hyeop-hoe). He continued his active role as jury and recommended artist of National Art Exhibition from 1949.\n In his early period, based on Academism, he mainly created landscape, still-life, and figure paintings. After the 1930s, his works had ‘local colors’ themes, depicting customs and lives of the people.\n This work was awarded a special prize from the Department of Eastern-style painting at the 15th Joseon Art Exhibition in 1936. This work, which has local colors, is one of Kim’s representative works during his prime. As a big-size four-panel folding screen, it was created for the exhibition when the artist worked in the Japanese Government-General of Korea. With a background of hanok, which is a traditional Korean house, women are preparing the meal, slicing and peeling vegetables. Enjoying spring sunshine, children look sleepy. Kim used various colors to highlight the beauty of hanbok, the traditional Korean clothing, as well as to give the liveliness to the screen. The detailed and realistic depictions of vegetables and stains on the door paper reveal the keen observation of the artist.": 1,
    "Jin, Hwan(1913-1951) left the Commercial Department of Bosung Professional School in 1931, and studied painting on his own. In 1934, he went to the Department of Western-style Painting at Japan School of Fine Arts(日本美術学校). While he was in the school, he continuously submitted to the outside exhibitions, such as the exhibition of Independent Art Association. He did not submit his works to the official exhibitions, such as Joseon Art Exhibition, and formed Joseon New Artist Association, the Western-style painters group, with several artists, including Lee, Jungsup(1916-1956) and Lee, Qoede(1913-1965). From 1948, he worked as a professor at Hongik University.\n Jin’s works embraces national atmosphere, and the main motif is cow with the ‘local colors.’\n This painting, one of Jin’s featuring works, has been considered to be submitted to the 3rd exhibition of Joseon New Artist Association. He painted abstractly the birds which seem to fly in the sky, the clouds, and the cows which seem to crouch in the simplified forms and tough brushwork. The used main color is ochre which Jin usually used. In a bird’s-eye view, Jin seemed to depict the imaginary place beyond the human’s visual experience.": 1,
    "Lee, Jungsup(pen name: Daehyang, 1916-1956) studied painting under Yim, Yongryun(b. 1901) at Jongro Public High School. He went to Imperial Art Institute(帝国美術学校), Japan in 1938, but he left the school and went to the Department of Art in Culture Institute(文化学院). He submitted his works to the exhibition of abstract art group in Tokyo, called Free Artists Association(自由美術家協会, Jiyu Bijutsuka Kyokai), and won the sun prize at the 7th exhibition in 1943. After returning home, he continuously produced works although he suffered from the diseases and financial difficulties.\n Inspired the traditional and folksy works, such as ancient mural paintings and folk paintings, Lee mainly painted cow and children, adopting Expressionism. During his study at Culture Institute, he dating with Yamamoto, Masako(山本方子) created the picture postcards whose images suggesting that they are a couple are surrealistic and mystical. During the refugee life in the Korean War, he depicted the happy days with the family. When he who was separated from the family took refuge alone in Busan, South Korea, he painted desolate landscape and war metaphors. After the Korean War, he was hopeful of being reunited with the family for a while, and the works have energetic atmosphere. However, his hope turned to dust since he was in debt, and he depicted dim landscapes.\n This piece is one of numerous illustrated letters which Lee sent to his family staying in Japan during the Korean War. In the letter, he wrote about his status and revealed his love for wife. The illustrations of his family convey the bright and hopeful atmosphere. As artwork, this letter that Lee’s calligraphy and drawings harmonize with each other has an important value not only as an artwork but as an archival material.": 1,
    "Lee, Yongwoo(pen name: Mukro, 1902-1952) was a member of Society of Painters and Calligraphers(西畵協會), which is the first modern art group in Korea. He submitted his works to Josoen Art Exhibition and Society of Painters and Calligraphers exhibition. In 1923, he formed the first traditional Korean painting group, the Society of Eastern Study(同硏社), with Lee, Sangbeom(1897-1972), No, Soohyeon(1899-1978), and Byeon, Gwansik(1899-1976). He served as the jury at National Art Exhibition.\n In the early 1930s, Lee painted landscape of mountain ideologically in his distinctive techniques. Around the 1940s, he tried to escape from the traditional style, and depicted Korea’s familiar scenery with ‘local colors’, such as farm village.\n This work, which was awarded at the 14th Joseon Art Exhibition, is only one existing among Lee’s works submitted to the exhibitions. It depicts the tranquil scenery in the mountain after the rain. He skilfully controled the amount of ink so that the rear mountains of the screen fade. This painting is his masterpiece which shows his improved techniques.": 1,
    "As a portrait painter active from the late Joseon era to the Japanese occupation of Korea, Chae Yongshin (1850‒1941) gained fame for his portrait of Emperor Gojong. Although he once served as governor of Jeongsan‒gun county, he retired from his post after the Eulsa Treaty of 1905, moving to the countryside to work as a portrait painter for patriotic figures of the late Joseon era, including Hwang Hyeon, Choi Ikhyeon, and Jeon Woo.\n  The right‒hand inscriptions in the Portrait of Jeon Woo reveal that the painting was created in 1911 to celebrate the 70th birthday of Jeon Woo (1841‒1922, known by the pen name “Ganjae”). Jeon Woo was one of the foremost Confucian scholars of the Wijeong Cheoksa proponents that sought to keep the nation closed to outside influences while upholding traditions. Once Korea was forcibly annexed by Japan, he traveled throughout the islands of Jeolla‒do, leading a life of seclusion while training young scholars. His determination to uphold neo‒Confucian traditions is readily apparent in his choice of garment, a long vestment in one piece (simui) reflecting the codes of decorum outlined in Juja garye (Chu Hsi’s Family Rituals). Capturing the look of stern vigor in his subject, who is squarely facing ahead, Chae conveys the upright character of this classical scholar. The artist aimed to reproduce even minute details seen in the delicately rendered moles and strands of facial hair. The portrait appears considerably modern given the photo‒like hyperrealism of the subject’s face, along with the three‒dimensional effect created by accentuated shadows.": 1
